 from a position of  strength I've always believed that once  they believe they could strike the  United States or threaten to strike us  that they can then enter these  negotiations on equal footing there is  another possibility one that I think is  less likely but maybe part of this as  well and that is that there's tremendous  pressure among elites in North Korea  including diplomats around the world who  are required to bring back ill found  gains and basically bribery stealing  money etc that there's a lot of pressure  and rumblings among them about  discontent with the direction of the  country and Kim jong-un feels pressure  to so some level of progress  with regards to that audience so it's  obviously this is a very mysterious  regime and so we don't have the amount  of insight into them as we would some  other country so what you're describing  senator is a lot of traps and a lot of  difficult things to be negotiated so  give us often give us your assessment of  the Trump administration so far because  for a lot of people this looks like a  tremendous breakthrough for the Trump  administration the president his  strategy so assess that what got us here  and whether you think the administration  has the capability to run all those  traps you just outline well there's two  one or two things have gotten us here  either number one the kim jungeun is  supremely confident that he now has  leverage over the United States because  he believes we believe that he can  strike us or be he's under some level of  pressure that threatens his regime and  he feels like he needs to show the  elites in his country that he is in a  strong position and that he is willing  to do things and give people hope that  things are going to get better  I have no disagreement with her approach  I don't think we would be at this point  today had it not been for sticking with  sanctions and I think they are right to  argue that nothing is going to change  just because there's going to be a 